<h2>1. Who we are, and who is responsible for your data</h2>
<p><strong>MyClash</strong> is a platform for managing, scoring and publishing Historical European Martial Arts (HEMA) competitions. It is developed and operated by:</p>
<blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>Association de Recherche et Développement des Arts Martiaux Historiques Européens à Lyon (LYON AMHE)</strong><br>
    Bâtiment 15, Résidence du Parc, 2 avenue Raymond de Veyssière, 69130 Écully, France<br>
    SIRET: 924 695 455 00015<br>
    Email: <a href="mailto:lyon.amhe@gmail.com">lyon.amhe@gmail.com</a>
  </p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hereinafter "<strong>we</strong>", "<strong>MyClash</strong>", or "<strong>the Association</strong>".</p>

<h3>1.1 Two different roles, depending on the data</h3>
<p>This distinction matters, because it determines who you should talk to:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Who is the data controller</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>You created a MyClash account</strong> (email address, password, profile, follows, notification preferences)</td>
      <td><strong>LYON AMHE</strong> — contact us directly</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>An event organiser entered you on their roster</strong> (competitor, referee, instructor, volunteer), whether or not you have an account</td>
      <td><strong>The organiser of that event.</strong> MyClash acts as their processor: we hold and process that data on their instructions</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>If your data reached MyClash through an event you took part in, the organiser of that event is your first point of contact. We will always help you reach them, and we act ourselves whenever the request concerns the platform.</p>

<!-- 2 -->
<h2>2. What data we hold</h2>
<p>The list below is not a summary written from memory: it mirrors the technical inventory the platform uses to build your data export (42 tables, grouped into the twelve files you receive). Anything we hold about you appears in one of these categories.</p>

<h3>2.1 Identity and profile</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>First name, last name, display name, alias</td><td>Required to appear on a roster and on results</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Email address</td><td>Required on an event roster; used for login and notifications. <strong>Never published.</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Password</td><td>Never stored in clear text — only a cryptographic hash held by our authentication service</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Date of birth, gender category</td><td>Optional; used for age and category eligibility</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Club, country</td><td>Displayed alongside results, as at the venue</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Profile photo</td><td>Optional, uploaded by you or by the organiser</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HEMA Ratings identifier</td><td>Optional; links your MyClash profile to your public HEMA Ratings profile</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Social and personal links, weapon disciplines, honours</td><td>Optional, entered by you</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notes taken by the organiser</td><td>Free text an organiser may attach to their own roster row</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>2.2 Competition record</h3>
<p>Registrations, pools, brackets, Swiss rounds, seeding, bouts fought, exchange-by-exchange scoring, penalties and cards received, forfeits, rankings, league standings and placements.</p>

<h3>2.3 Roles at an event</h3>
<p>Referee qualifications and per-role ratings, availability, assignments to pistes and bouts, referee compensation and payment records, workshop instruction and enrolment, feedback given on workshops.</p>

<h3>2.4 Account activity</h3>
<p>Organisation memberships and roles, the people and organisers you follow, your directory groups, notification preferences, browser push subscriptions, requests you make (profile claim, email change, correction of an exchange, league membership, club review), and your privacy settings.</p>

<h3>2.5 Record of your agreement to our documents</h3>
<p>When you create an account, or continue as a guest, we record <strong>which version</strong> of the Terms of Service and of this Privacy Policy was displayed to you, <strong>when</strong> you accepted it, and the <strong>IP address and browser</strong> the acceptance came from. We are required to be able to demonstrate consent, and a timestamp alone would not do that. These records are never modified — accepting a revised version adds a new record beside the old one.</p>

<h3>2.6 Assistance features (AI)</h3>
<p>If an organiser has enabled the AI features (see §7), the questions asked and the content generated are recorded, together with the volume consumed. See §8 for what leaves our infrastructure.</p>

<h3>2.7 Technical data</h3>
<p>The IP address and browser of a guest session and of an acceptance record, server logs, and error reports. <strong>We do not perform audience measurement</strong>: MyClash contains no analytics tool, no advertising tag, and no third-party tracker. We do not record which pages you visit or how long you spend on them.</p>

<h3>2.8 What we never collect</h3>
<p>No special-category data within the meaning of Article 9 GDPR (health, ethnic origin, political or religious opinions, biometric data), no bank or payment details, and no precise geolocation.</p>

<!-- 3 -->
<h2>3. Being on MyClash without ever having signed up</h2>
<p>This is the most distinctive part of the platform, and it deserves a section of its own.</p>
<p>To run a competition, an organiser enters their roster: everyone taking part, with the details listed in §2.1. <strong>You can therefore exist on MyClash without ever having created an account</strong>, because the organiser needed to register you in order to run their event.</p>
<p>In that situation:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You may claim your profile.</strong> A link sent to the email address the organiser registered proves it is you, and the profile becomes yours.</li>
  <li><strong>You may use a guest session.</strong> Finding your own name on an event's roster opens a limited session — your schedule, your bouts, your workshops — with no account and no password. It is a cookie tied to that event which expires seven days after the event ends.</li>
  <li><strong>You may object.</strong> Write to the organiser, or to us; see §11.</li>
</ul>

<!-- 4 -->
<h2>4. Why we hold this data</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Legal basis (Art. 6 GDPR)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Running the competition: registrations, draws, scoring, rankings, results</td><td>Performance of a contract (the organiser's), and our legitimate interest in operating the platform</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Creating and managing your account</td><td>Performance of a contract (the Terms of Service)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Publishing results (see §5)</td><td>Legitimate interest: a competition is public, and its results are its purpose</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notifications about your bouts and your schedule</td><td>Your consent, per notification type, revocable in your settings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Platform security, abuse and fraud prevention, audit trail</td><td>Legitimate interest</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Demonstrating acceptance of our documents</td><td>Legal obligation (Art. 7(1) GDPR)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Answering your requests</td><td>Legal obligation (Art. 15 to 22 GDPR)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>We send no marketing.</strong> MyClash has no newsletter and no promotional email. The only messages we send concern your account or an event you are taking part in.</p>

<!-- 5 -->
<h2>5. What is public, and what never is</h2>
<p>Our rule is simple, and it is written down as a project decision: <strong>what is public online is what would be visible at the venue.</strong> A HEMA competition is public — names are announced, brackets are on the wall, results are read out.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Visibility</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Name, club, country</td><td>Public on results and brackets</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Scores, rankings, bracket position, penalties received</td><td>Public</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Exchange-by-exchange detail</td><td>Public at event level; aggregated in statistics</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Profile photo</td><td>Visible to signed-in users; an organiser may make an event fully public</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Your personal schedule, your workshop attendance</td><td>Visible only to you and to the organiser</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Email address</strong></td><td><strong>Never public.</strong> No public interface returns it; the database itself enforces this</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Date of birth, notes, technical data</td><td>Never public</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>You can restrict some of this yourself in your settings: hide your workshops, refuse to be followed, keep your email hidden from the people who follow you. <strong>Publication of your results is not currently a self-service setting</strong> — if you want your name withdrawn from public results, ask us and we will anonymise it (see §11).</p>

<!-- 6 -->
<h2>6. How long we keep it</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Retention</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Account and profile</td><td>For as long as the account exists</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Guest sessions</td><td><strong>90 days</strong>, then automatic deletion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notification delivery records</td><td><strong>365 days</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>AI usage records</td><td><strong>365 days</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Administrative audit trail</td><td>Kept — it is our record of who did what; personal data inside it is redacted when an account is erased</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Competition results</td><td>Kept, as the historical record of a public competition (see below)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Error reports</td><td>Per our error-tracking provider's retention (see §7)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>These durations are enforced automatically by the platform, not applied by hand.</p>

<h3>6.1 What happens when you delete your account</h3>
<p><strong>Deletion is immediate. There is no waiting period and no way to reverse it.</strong> The moment you confirm:</p>
<ul>
  <li>your login credentials are destroyed and you can no longer sign in;</li>
  <li>your profile details — email, date of birth, photo, links, notes — are erased;</li>
  <li>your guest sessions, push subscriptions, follows and preferences are deleted;</li>
  <li>personal data inside the audit trail is redacted in place.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Your competition results keep your name.</strong> A result is the record of a public competition in which you took part, alongside the opponents you faced; erasing it would rewrite other people's history too. If you want your name removed from published results as well, ask us: we anonymise it, and the result survives under an anonymous entry.</p>
<p>We retain a record that an erasure took place and when — never who it concerned. That record is a one-way cryptographic fingerprint, which cannot be traced back to you.</p>

<!-- 7 -->
<h2>7. Who else processes your data</h2>
<p>We never sell your personal data. The following providers process it on our behalf, each strictly for the purpose listed:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Role</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>OVHcloud</strong> (France)</td><td>Server hosting: the application, the database, uploaded files</td><td>🇪🇺 France</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Scaleway</strong> (France)</td><td>Off-site storage of encrypted backups</td><td>🇪🇺 France</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Resend</strong> (Netherlands)</td><td>Sending our emails: sign-in links, account emails, event notifications. Receives your email address and the content of the message</td><td>🇪🇺 European Union</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Sentry</strong> — Functional Software, Inc. (United States)</td><td>Error reporting. Receives <strong>application error logs only</strong> — see §7.1</td><td>🇺🇸 United States</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Push services</strong> — Google, Mozilla, Apple, Microsoft depending on your browser</td><td>Delivering push notifications, <strong>only if you enable them</strong>. Receives the notification content and your browser's subscription address</td><td>🌍 Varies by browser</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>AI providers</strong> — Anthropic, OpenAI, Mistral AI, Google</td><td><strong>Optional, and off by default.</strong> Only where an organiser has supplied their own key does the content of their requests reach the chosen provider</td><td>🇺🇸 United States · 🇪🇺 France (Mistral)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Two components frequently mistaken for external services are <strong>not</strong> ones: our database and authentication (Supabase) and our file storage run <strong>on our own European servers</strong>, in self-hosted mode. No data reaches Supabase, Inc.</p>

<h3>7.1 What Sentry does and does not receive</h3>
<p>Sentry receives a technical error log: the fault, the line of code that produced it, the version of the application, and the sequence of pages that led there. It is the tool that tells us the application is broken before you have to.</p>
<p><strong>We have explicitly disabled the collection of personal data by this tool.</strong> Concretely, the following are <strong>never</strong> transmitted:</p>
<ul>
  <li>your IP address;</li>
  <li>your cookies and your session;</li>
  <li>the content of what you submit (forms, scores, messages);</li>
  <li>your identity — we never attach an account to an error report.</li>
</ul>
<p>On top of that, the diagnostic details our servers add to a report pass through a filter that removes email addresses, authentication tokens and any value bearing the name of a secret.</p>
<p>One detail, for completeness: an error log contains the <strong>address of the page</strong> where it occurred, and some MyClash addresses include the name of a public profile — for instance <code>/fighters/jean-dupont</code>. That information is the same as what the page itself displays publicly. Nothing private travels with it.</p>

<h3>7.2 HEMA Ratings</h3>
<p>MyClash <strong>reads</strong> public ratings from <a href="https://hemaratings.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hemaratings.com</a> to enrich fighter profiles. We send nothing automatically. An organiser may export their results in the HEMA Ratings format, but it is <strong>the organiser</strong> who chooses to submit that file, under their own responsibility.</p>

<h3>7.3 Legal authorities</h3>
<p>We may have to disclose data to the competent authorities upon lawful request, or to defend our rights.</p>

<!-- 8 -->
<h2>8. Transfers outside the European Union</h2>
<p>Our infrastructure — application, database, files, backups — is hosted <strong>entirely in France</strong>. Three flows nonetheless leave the European Union, and we state them plainly rather than claiming otherwise:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Flow</th><th>Destination</th><th>Safeguard</th><th>Can you avoid it?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Application error logs</td><td>Sentry, United States</td><td>Standard Contractual Clauses and the EU–US Data Privacy Framework</td><td>Not individually — but see §7.1: these logs carry no personal data</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Push notifications</td><td>Your browser's push service</td><td>The provider's own framework</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — do not enable notifications, or revoke them</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AI requests</td><td>The provider chosen by the organiser</td><td>Standard Contractual Clauses. Mistral AI is French, so that option stays within the EU</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — the feature is off unless an organiser enables it</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!-- 9 -->
<h2>9. Cookies</h2>
<p>MyClash sets <strong>four cookies, all our own</strong>. None is used for advertising, audience measurement or tracking.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Cookie</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Lifetime</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>sb-access-token</code></td><td>Keeps you signed in</td><td>Session, renewed while you stay active</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sb-refresh-token</code></td><td>Renews your session without asking you to sign in again</td><td>30 days</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>mc_guest</code></td><td>Your guest session at an event, when you have no account</td><td>Until 7 days after the event</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>mc_locale</code></td><td>Remembers whether you chose French or English</td><td>1 year</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>This is why you will not see a cookie banner.</strong> A consent banner is required for cookies that are not necessary — advertising, analytics, third-party trackers. MyClash sets none. The first three cookies are strictly necessary for a service you asked for by signing in; the fourth records a choice you made yourself. Displaying a consent dialog that decides nothing would only teach you to dismiss the ones that matter.</p>
<p>If MyClash ever adds a measurement tool, this section changes and a real consent mechanism will come with it — one that blocks the tool until you agree.</p>

<!-- 10 -->
<h2>10. Security</h2>
<ul>
  <li>All traffic is encrypted in transit (HTTPS/TLS).</li>
  <li>Passwords are never stored in clear text.</li>
  <li>Access to your data is enforced by the database itself, not merely by the application.</li>
  <li>Session cookies are inaccessible to JavaScript, which neutralises an entire class of attack.</li>
  <li>Backups are encrypted and stored off-site.</li>
  <li>Sign-in attempts are rate-limited, and administrative actions are recorded in an audit trail.</li>
  <li>MyClash is open-source software: its security can be inspected by anyone rather than taken on trust.</li>
</ul>

<!-- 11 -->
<h2>11. Your rights</h2>
<p>Under the GDPR (Articles 15 to 22) you have the following rights. Several are available directly in the application, without waiting for us:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Right</th><th>How to exercise it</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Access and portability</strong> (Art. 15 and 20)</td><td><strong>Immediate.</strong> Settings → download your data. You receive a complete archive of everything we hold, in a machine-readable format</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Erasure</strong> (Art. 17)</td><td><strong>Immediate.</strong> Settings → delete my account. See §6.1</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Rectification</strong> (Art. 16)</td><td>Editable in your profile. For an event roster, ask the organiser or write to us</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Objection</strong> (Art. 21)</td><td>Some options in your settings. To have your name withdrawn from published results, write to us and we will anonymise it</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Restriction</strong> (Art. 18)</td><td>Write to us</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Withdrawal of consent</strong></td><td>Notifications are revocable at any time in your settings, with no effect on what came before</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>For anything not self-service: <strong><a href="mailto:lyon.amhe@gmail.com">lyon.amhe@gmail.com</a></strong>. We answer within one month (extendable to three for complex requests, with prior notice).</p>
<p>If your request concerns an event roster, the organiser of that event is the controller (see §1.1). Write to us anyway if you cannot reach them — we will not leave you without a way through.</p>
<p>You also have the right to lodge a complaint with the CNIL: <a href="https://www.cnil.fr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">www.cnil.fr</a> — 3 Place de Fontenoy, 75007 Paris, France.</p>

<!-- 12 -->
<h2>12. Changes to this policy</h2>
<p>Each version of this policy carries a date, and MyClash records which version you accepted. When we publish a materially changed version, the application <strong>tells you and asks you to accept the new one</strong> — you are not locked out of your account in the meantime, particularly not in the middle of a competition.</p>
<p>You can see at any time which version you accepted, and when, in your account settings.</p>

